# Line endings for files that are executed rather than read.
#
# A shell script is parsed by its interpreter byte for byte, and a carriage return is not
# whitespace to it -- it is part of the token. `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r` asks the kernel for an
# interpreter literally named "bash\r", which does not exist, and the failure surfaces as
#
#     /usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory
#
# with the container exiting 127 before a single line of the application runs.
#
# That is not hypothetical. Building this image on a Windows checkout produced exactly that:
# git's `core.autocrlf=true` rewrote docker/entrypoint.sh to CRLF on checkout, the Dockerfile
# COPYed it in byte for byte, and the image could not start. It passed CI the whole time,
# because a Linux runner checks the same commit out with LF -- so the defect was invisible
# to every automated check and reachable only by a contributor on Windows.
#
# `text eol=lf` says: normalise to LF in the repository, and check out LF *on every platform*.
# The blobs were already LF; what was missing was the instruction not to convert them on the
# way out.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The Gradle wrapper is a shell script without the extension, and CI runs it as `bash ./gradlew`.
gradlew text eol=lf

# Executed on the head unit by toybox sh after being base64'd across adb -- the same failure,
# somewhere far harder to see, because it would come back as a sampler that silently never
# produced a line rather than as a build that failed.
backend/app/ingest/carplay_timing.sh text eol=lf

# Read by Docker, which is tolerant of CRLF, but keeping it LF avoids a spurious whole-file
# diff whenever a Windows and a Linux checkout touch it in turn.
Dockerfile text eol=lf
docker/*.sh text eol=lf
